    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media across the spectrum agree that the “Rente mit 63” proposal is financially untenable without a clearly defined transition period and that any early‑retirement measure must be funded in a way that does not increase the burden on younger workers facing demographic shrinkage. At the same time there is a shared recognition that pension poverty, especially among women and former East‑German retirees, is a pressing social issue that any reform must address through stronger safety nets or supplemental private savings. Finally, commentators from liberal, conservative and left‑leaning outlets converge on the view that sustainable pension policy requires a balance between inter‑generational fairness and maintaining a competitive economy, even if they differ on the exact mix of public versus capital‑funded components.",
      "controversial_points": "The debate pits intergenerational equity against fiscal sustainability of the pay‑as‑you‑go system. Conservatives frame early retirement as a social right; left parties see it as wealth redistribution harming younger workers. Economic liberals stress market‑based capital pensions and argue that state subsidies distort competition and burden taxpayers. Regional divides surface as eastern politicians demand protection against pension cuts, while western parties emphasize national fiscal discipline."
